mode: "100644"
text: "# Formats & Audio on Reels\n\nPick the format that fits the idea and the creator's comfort, then decide the audio. Both are\nReels-specific choices a generic \"video script\" misses — and audio on Instagram comes with a hard\npublishing limit worth knowing up front.\n\n## Reels formats\n\n- **Talking head** — a person to camera, with energy. Best for opinions, tips, story, hot takes;\n  builds the face-recognition that converts profile visits to follows. Delivery craft →\n  `talking-head-and-piece-to-camera`.\n- **Tutorial / how-to** — screen-record or demo, numbered steps. The save-and-send workhorse; strong\n  for Instagram search.\n- **Transformation / before-after** — show the after in the first frame, then earn it. High\n  watch-through when the arc is real (no fabricated results).\n- **Listicle** — \"3 tools that…\". Fast, dense, rewatchable; loop fuel.\n- **POV / relatable skit** — the shared-experience clip people DM to a specific friend; the purest\n  sends format.\n- **B-roll + text overlay** — aesthetic footage carrying an on-screen-text essay; fully sound-off\n  native, low production cost.\n- **Faceless / voiceover** — b-roll or AI visuals + voiceover. For niches, shy creators, or scaled\n  output (where the Veo pack earns its place — see `veo-prompt-pack.md`).\n\nMatch format to the message *and* to who's making it: don't script a to-camera confession for\nsomeone who won't deliver it, or a faceless overlay when a founder's face would build more trust.\n\n## Audio strategy\n\nThree choices — and one rule that overrides all of them: **the Reel must work on mute** (most\nviewers never unmute; see `reels-mechanics.md`).\n\n1. **Original audio** — your own talking/voiceover. The default for founder/brand content,\n   tutorials, and opinions; it's also what Instagram search transcribes (put the keyword in the\n   first spoken seconds — `instagram-seo`).\n2. **Trending audio** — a rising track can lend a lift and signals \"native,\" when it genuinely fits\n   the mood or format. Business/creator accounts face a **smaller commercial-safe library** than\n   personal accounts — check what the account can actually use.\n3. **Music bed** — a licensed/commercial-safe track for vibe under a voiceover.\n\n### The native-only limit (don't promise otherwise)\n\nInstagram's licensed music library is **only attachable in-app**. The script **names the audio\ndirection** (\"trending upbeat track, cuts on the beat\" or a specific sound if one is known); the\n**human adds the track natively** when publishing or reviewing the scheduled post. WoopSocial\npublishes the video, caption, and cover — it cannot attach IG library audio, and this skill never\nclaims it can. (Publish mechanics → `instagram-reels-publishing`.)\n\n### The fit test — don't force a trend\n\nA trending audio must serve the message and suit the audience; forcing a meme sound onto a B2B\ncompliance explainer reads as try-hard and hurts trust. If a trend doesn't fit, use original audio\nor a format-level trend (a structure, a template) that does. Live trend research and timing →\n`trend-jacking` — this skill rides a sound where it fits; it doesn't chase the trend feed.\n\n## Beat sync\n\nWhen a track drives the edit, sync cuts and text reveals to its beats — the familiarity does some of\nthe scroll-stopping. Note the sync in the production doc (\"cut lands on the drop at 0:07\").\n\n## Output note\n\nIn the production doc, name the **audio choice** (original / trending / music bed), flag **native\nadd-in-app** where it applies, and note **beat sync** when it matters. That's the Reels-native\ndetail a generic script leaves out.\n"
